@@ -0,0 +1,64 @@
+# UI sounds: playing on time
+
+## Why they played late
+
+`UiSounds.play` made a new `Sound`, played it, and destroyed it when it ended. Roblox keeps a
+sound's audio in memory only while some `Sound` instance references that asset. Once the cue was
+destroyed, nothing referenced the asset, so the next cue had to load it again before it could
+play. That's why `IsLoaded` was `false` at play time and the click came 0.3 to 0.5 s late, worse
+on phones with slower storage and network.
+
+`ContentProvider:PreloadAsync` at startup didn't help. It loads the assets once, but it doesn't
+keep a reference to them, so they could be dropped again before the first cue.
+
+## What changed
+
+- `UiSounds` (`src/StarterPlayer/StarterPlayerScripts/UiSounds.luau`) now has a counted sound
+  cache:
+  - `UiSounds.hold(soundId)` keeps one `Sound` with that id in `SoundService.UiSoundCache` and
+    returns a release function.
+  - Holds are counted. Screens that play the same sound share one cached copy, and the copy is
+    destroyed only when the last holder releases it.
+  - `UiSounds.play(soundId)` clones the cached copy, which is already loaded, so it starts at once.
+  - When nothing holds the id, `play` falls back to the old behavior (a fresh Sound that plays
+    late). In Studio it also warns `UiSounds: <id> played with no hold`, so a new screen that
+    forgot its hold shows up in the output.
+  - `UiSounds.isCached(soundId)` reports whether an id is cached right now.
+  - Holds do nothing outside a running game, so requiring the module in edit mode creates nothing.
+- Who holds what:
+  - The HUD (`app` in `Main.client.luau`) holds `Click` and `PickerOpen` for its whole life. The
+    Build button, the tiles, and later screens all play `Click`. `PickerOpen` plays the moment
+    build mode turns on, which can be at any time, so it has to be loaded before that happens.
+  - The block picker holds `PickerClose` from its component body, so the cached copy exists only
+    while the picker is open and is released after its close cue plays.
+- The `PreloadAsync` call in `Main.client.luau` is gone.
+
+A new screen holds its own sounds from its component body, with
+`cleanup(UiSounds.hold(SoundIds.X))`, and leaves `Click` out because the HUD already holds it.
+
+## Checking it in Studio
+
+1. Start a playtest (Play, not Run, so there is a client). A load in Studio is usually faster
+   than on a phone, so judge by `IsLoaded` in step 4 rather than by ear. Confirm the timing on a
+   real phone afterward.
+2. In the Explorer, open `SoundService > UiSoundCache`. While the picker is closed it should hold
+   `Click` and `PickerOpen`. Press Build: `PickerClose` should appear. Press Build again:
+   `PickerClose` should be gone a moment later.
+3. Press Build and tap tiles. The click and the open sound should play right away, and the open
+   sound should come with the picker, not after it.
+4. To check `IsLoaded` at the moment a cue plays, run this in the command bar on the **client**
+   while the playtest runs, then press Build and tap a tile:
+
+   ```lua
+   game:GetService("SoundService").ChildAdded:Connect(function(child)
+   	if child:IsA("Sound") then
+   		print(child.SoundId, "IsLoaded at play:", child.IsLoaded)
+   	end
+   end)
+   ```
+
+   Every line should say `true`. Before this change they said `false`.
+5. The output should show no `UiSounds: ... played with no hold` warnings.
+
+Known gap: `PickerClose` starts loading when the picker opens. If the picker is closed within a
+fraction of a second of opening, before the load finishes, that one close cue can still be late.
